+# R-03: Ambiguous requirement record
+
+## Title
+
+"Marketing wants to know where clicks come from."
+
+## Source
+
+| Field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Origin | The assignment brief, ambiguous scenario |
+| Design sections | docs/design/2026-10-12-system-design.md#1.2 (FR-15), #1.4, #6.3; ADR 0006 |
+| Status | draft; normalized by T-201 |
+| Questions written on | 2026-10-12 |
+
+## Normalized problem statement
+
+Not yet normalized. The sentence does not say which dimension "where" means, who consumes the answer, over what time window, or whether storing anything about the visitor is acceptable. The design deliberately does not pre-decide it. T-201 records dated questions, dated answers, the candidate directions with cost and privacy implications, and the decision; this section is rewritten there. The constraint already fixed is the privacy posture of ADR 0006: no IP addresses and no raw user-agent strings are persisted.
+
+## Functional requirements covered
+
+- FR-15: outcome determined by T-201 and recorded here; T-202 implements it, T-203 tests and documents it.
+
+## Non-functional requirements covered
+
+- NFR-06 privacy: binds any direction that touches IP or user agent.
+- NFR-03 reliability: any attribution value must be captured on the request thread into the immutable event; nothing may delay the redirect beyond a queue offer.
+- NFR-10 change safety: if a migration is needed it is V3, single-column `ALTER` statements only.
+- NFR-02 performance: the redirect path stays one indexed read plus a queue offer.
+
+## Ambiguities identified and reading adopted
+
+| Ambiguity | Candidate readings | Reading adopted |
+|---|---|---|
+| "Where" | Referring site; campaign parameters on the destination URL; geography derived from IP; device or browser family; time-of-day patterns | pending T-201 |
+| "Marketing wants to know" | Field in the stats API response; export; dashboard | pending T-201 |
+| Time window | All time; last 24 hours and 7 days like FR-08; configurable | pending T-201 |
+| Storage | Nothing new; a derived low-cardinality value per click; a new table | pending T-201 |
+| Privacy | Keep ADR 0006 as is; amend it with a new ADR for a derived, non-identifying value | pending T-201 |
+
+## Stakeholder clarification log
+
+The engineer plays the stakeholder. Questions carry their own timestamp; answers will carry theirs, later than the question they answer.
+
+| Timestamp (ISO 8601 with offset) | Who | Question or answer |
+|---|---|---|
+| 2026-10-12T06:45:00+05:30 | Engineer | Q1: Which attribution dimension matters most: referring site, campaign, geography, or device? |
+| 2026-10-12T06:45:00+05:30 | Engineer | Q2: Is storing client IP addresses or derived location acceptable? The current privacy posture stores no IP and no raw user agent. |
+| 2026-10-12T06:45:00+05:30 | Engineer | Q3: Should the breakdown be in the stats API response, an export, or a dashboard, and over what time window? |
+| pending | Engineer as stakeholder | Answers: pending, to be recorded with their own timestamp before T-202 starts |
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- No real marketing team exists; the dated question-before-answer record is the evidence of clarification (#1.4).
+- At least one answer removes scope, otherwise the clarification did no work.
+- Any direction that stores a value derived from IP or user agent needs an ADR amending 0006 before T-202.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Provisional until T-201 decides: raw IP or raw user-agent storage; third-party analytics services; real-time dashboards.
+- Server-side fetching of destination pages to infer anything.
+
+## Tasks
+
+T-201 normalization and decision; T-202 implementation of the decided slice, high impact if it adds V3; T-203 tests, docs, decision record update.
+
+## Validation
+
+- This record shows every question timestamped earlier than its answer, and a filled decision with the normalized EARS criteria and test names.
+- T-202's acceptance criteria are copied from the decision before its prompt v1 is committed and pass in the default build.
+- docs/scenarios/ambiguous.md shows normalization, decomposition, execution evidence links, and validation.
